ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_9:002,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_9,2,"informed decisions that lead to improved productivity, pro␀itability, and sustainability. Let us move forward together to build a resilient, prosperous, and empowered farming community #Viksit Bharat 2047. Jai Kisan, Jai Vigyan! At ICAR, we remain committed to the vision of a knowledge-driven, inclusive, and sustainable agricultural system. The advisories compiled herein re␀lect our continuous eﬀorts to translate scienti␀ic knowledge from laboratories into actionable insights for every farmer. I urge extension of␀icers, KVK teams, farmer producer organizations (FPOs), and progressive farmers to widely disseminate and implement the recommendations provided in this book. (M.L. Jat) It is with great pleasure that I present the “ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025”- a timely and comprehensive guide developed to empower India's farming community with science-based, region-speci␀ic advisories for the upcoming Kharif season. This publication marks a signi␀icant step toward strengthening climate- resilient agriculture and enhancing farm productivity across diverse agro-climatic zones. Dr. M.L. Jat Secretary (DARE) & Director General (ICAR) (v) lR;eso t;rs",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_10:001,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_10,1,"I congratulate the scientists, editors, and extension professionals involved in compiling this publication. I also encourage ␀ield functionaries and stakeholders to use this resource extensively to support farmers during the Kharif season. Let us continue to work together to transform Indian agriculture into a more resilient, productive, and sustainable sector. This book compiles region-speci␀ic advisories that re␀lect the latest research and ␀ield-level insights from ICAR institutes, Agricultural Universities, and Krishi Vigyan Kendras (KVKs) across the country. It is designed to serve as a practical guide for extension personnel, progressive farmers, farmer producer organizations (FPOs), and agri-entrepreneurs engaged in Kharif crop planning and management. The advisories encompass timely, location-speci␀ic, and scienti␀ically backed recommendations and cover major crops, livestock, and ␀isheries, with a focus on improving productivity, pro␀itability, and sustainability. At ICAR, we believe that technology transfer and knowledge dissemination are as vital as research itself. The role of the extension system, particularly KVKs, has been pivotal in bridging the gap between research",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_10:002,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_10,2,"institutions and farming communities. This book is a result of collaborative eﬀorts between research and extension systems and exempli␀ies our commitment to farmer-centric innovation. (Rajbir Singh) India's agricultural landscape is rich in diversity, yet increasingly in␀luenced by climatic uncertainties, resource constraints, and changing socio-economic dynamics. To address these challenges eﬀectively, there is a growing need to equip farmers with timely, localized, and scienti␀ically validated information that enhances their decision-making capacity. This compilation “ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025” is a well-conceived eﬀort in this direction. Dr. Rajbir Singh Deputy Director General (Agricultural Extension), ICAR (vii) PREFACE",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_11:001,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_11,1,"I congratulate the scientists, editors, and extension professionals involved in compiling this publication. I also encourage ␀ield functionaries and stakeholders to use this resource extensively to support farmers during the Kharif season. Let us continue to work together to transform Indian agriculture into a more resilient, productive, and sustainable sector. This book compiles region-speci␀ic advisories that re␀lect the latest research and ␀ield-level insights from ICAR institutes, Agricultural Universities, and Krishi Vigyan Kendras (KVKs) across the country. It is designed to serve as a practical guide for extension personnel, progressive farmers, farmer producer organizations (FPOs), and agri-entrepreneurs engaged in Kharif crop planning and management. The advisories encompass timely, location-speci␀ic, and scienti␀ically backed recommendations and cover major crops, livestock, and ␀isheries, with a focus on improving productivity, pro␀itability, and sustainability. At ICAR, we believe that technology transfer and knowledge dissemination are as vital as research itself. The role of the extension system, particularly KVKs, has been pivotal in bridging the gap between research",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_11:002,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_11,2,"institutions and farming communities. This book is a result of collaborative eﬀorts between research and extension systems and exempli␀ies our commitment to farmer-centric innovation. (Rajbir Singh) India's agricultural landscape is rich in diversity, yet increasingly in␀luenced by climatic uncertainties, resource constraints, and changing socio-economic dynamics. To address these challenges eﬀectively, there is a growing need to equip farmers with timely, localized, and scienti␀ically validated information that enhances their decision-making capacity. This compilation “ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025” is a well-conceived eﬀort in this direction. Dr. Rajbir Singh Deputy Director General (Agricultural Extension), ICAR (vii) PREFACE",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_12:001,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_12,1,"INTRODUCTION To mitigate these risks, it is imperative to provide farmers with location-speci␀ic, research-backed guidance. Agro-advisories play a pivotal role by oﬀering timely, region-speci␀ic, science-based information on weather patterns, crop management, pest control, harvesting, and storage. These advisories integrate seasonal outlooks with crop calendars and state-wise sowing windows, enabling farmers to select appropriate crop varieties and sowing dates, optimize resource use, and prevent yield losses. The Kharif season witnesses the cultivation of a diverse array of crops across India's agro-climatic zones, including rice, maize, cotton, soybean, groundnut, bajra, jowar, tur, urad, and moong. Despite advancements in agricultural technology, the sector remains vulnerable to climatic ␀luctuations such as delayed rains, uneven distribution, and extreme weather events. These challenges can adversely aﬀect crop yields, farm incomes, and rural livelihoods. This advisory serves as a crucial tool for agricultural of␀icers, Farmer Producer Organizations (FPOs), and NGOs involved in rural development. By bridging the information gap between research and practice, it empowers farmers",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_12:002,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_12,2,"with the knowledge needed to make informed decisions, thereby contributing to the vision of a self-reliant, prosperous, and inclusive India @ VIKSIT BHARAT 2047. Agriculture remains the cornerstone of India's economy, supporting nearly half of the population and providing employment, nutrition, and rural development. The Kharif season, spanning from June to October, is particularly vital due to its reliance on the south-west monsoon. Approximately 55% of India's net sown area is rainfed, making the timely and equitable distribution of monsoon rains crucial for agricultural success. In 2025, the India Meteorological Department (IMD) forecasts an above-normal monsoon season, with cumulative rainfall expected at 105% of the Long Period Average (LPA) of 87 cm. The monsoon's onset is anticipated to be slightly delayed in southern India but should reach Maharashtra by May 27, facilitating timely sowing preparations. The IMD's forecast of an above-normal monsoon oﬀers a promising outlook but still carries risks of intra-seasonal dry spells and localized ␀looding. The Indian government's initiatives, such as the ""Panchayat Mausam Seva,"" aim to deliver hyper-local weather forecasts at the",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_12:003,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_12,3,"panchayat level, ensuring that farmers receive accurate and timely information. (ix)",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_13:001,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_13,1,"INTRODUCTION To mitigate these risks, it is imperative to provide farmers with location-speci␀ic, research-backed guidance. Agro-advisories play a pivotal role by oﬀering timely, region-speci␀ic, science-based information on weather patterns, crop management, pest control, harvesting, and storage. These advisories integrate seasonal outlooks with crop calendars and state-wise sowing windows, enabling farmers to select appropriate crop varieties and sowing dates, optimize resource use, and prevent yield losses. The Kharif season witnesses the cultivation of a diverse array of crops across India's agro-climatic zones, including rice, maize, cotton, soybean, groundnut, bajra, jowar, tur, urad, and moong. Despite advancements in agricultural technology, the sector remains vulnerable to climatic ␀luctuations such as delayed rains, uneven distribution, and extreme weather events. These challenges can adversely aﬀect crop yields, farm incomes, and rural livelihoods. This advisory serves as a crucial tool for agricultural of␀icers, Farmer Producer Organizations (FPOs), and NGOs involved in rural development. By bridging the information gap between research and practice, it empowers farmers",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
