ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_65:002,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_65,2,"Gujarat Groundnut SOUTH SAURASHTRA Gujarat provides region-speci␀ic, science-based recommendations to help farmers improve productivity and resilience during the monsoon season. With climate variability and resource constraints in mind, it promotes integrated crop management, timely pest-disease control, ef␀icient water-nutrient use, and adoption of improved seed varieties. Tailored to the state's varied soils and rainfall patterns, this guidance aims to support sustainable farming and secure farmer livelihoods across Gujarat. This region includes Amreli, Bhavnagar, Botad, Devbhumi-Dwarka, Gir-Somnath, Jamnagar, Junagadh, Morbi, Porbandar, Rajkot, and Surendranagar, SAU: Junagarh Agricultural University, Junagarh (Gujarat). They have an average rainfall of 400- 1000 mm. Soils are shallow to medium black soils, often aﬀected by salinity and alkalinity. Groundnut cultivation begins with deep ploughing the ␀ield 2-3 times to achieve a ␀ine tilth up to 25-30 cm depth. Incorporating 5-7 t/ha of well-decomposed farmyard manure (FYM) before sowing improves soil fertility. Sowing is generally done during June-July. Recommended semi-spreading varieties include GJG-22, GG-20, and Girnar-4,",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_65:003,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_65,3,"while erect types include GJG-32, GJG-17, and KDG-128. For optimal plant spacing, maintain 60 × 10 cm for semi-spreading types and 45 × 10 cm for erect ones. Seeds should be treated with Vitavax and Rhizobium at 375-500 g/ha to promote healthy germination and nitrogen ␀ixation. Weed control involves pre-emergence application of Pendimethalin at 1.0 kg/ha, followed by two hand weedings at 20 and 40 days after sowing (DAS), or alternatively, post-emergence application of Quizalofop-P-ethyl at 50 g/ha. Timely irrigation is crucial, especially during ␀lowering, pegging, and pod development stages in case of dry spells. 4948",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_66:001,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_66,1,"Gujarat ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisory 2025 Nutrient management includes application of potassium at 50 kg/ha along with recommended nitrogen and phosphorus doses, and in saline soils, gypsum should be applied at 5 t/ha. To enhance crop nutrition, foliar spray of 0.2% boric acid or nano boron should be applied at 30, 45, and 60 DAS. For plant protection, collar and stem rot can be managed with a spray of Mancozeb or neem extract around 75 DAS. White grub infestation can be controlled through seed treatment with Chlorpyriphos or Imidacloprid, and soil application of Metarhizium anisopliae or Carbofuran. Sucking pests should be managed through seed treatment with Imidacloprid or foliar spray with Thiamethoxam, while defoliators can be controlled using Quinalphos, Chlorpyriphos, or Flubendiamide. Harvesting should be done at 120-125 DAS using a blade harrow, followed by threshing to separate the pods. For weed management, Pendimethalin is applied pre-emergence and Imazethapyr post-emergence, along with hand weeding at 20 and 40 days after sowing (DAS). To manage sucking pests, seed treatment with Imidacloprid is eﬀective, while collar rot can be controlled by treating seeds with",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_66:002,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_66,2,"Fluxapyroxad or a combination of Carboxin and Thiram. In case of defoliator infestation, spraying Indoxacarb, Lambda- cyhalothrin, or a Novaluron mixture is recommended. For rust and leaf spot diseases, fungicides like Hexaconazole or Pyraclostrobin should be applied. Additionally, 75% of the recommended dose of fertilizers (RDF) should be supplemented with 25 kg/ha of Mycorrhiza and Rhizobium seed treatment to enhance soil health and crop productivity. Soybean grows best in well-drained loamy soils and should be avoided in saline or alkaline conditions. Deep ploughing and the application of farmyard manure (FYM) are essential for soil preparation. Recommended varieties include JS 335, GJS-3, and GS-4. Sowing is done in June-July with a spacing of 45×5 cm and a depth of 3-5 cm, using a seed rate of 70-80 kg/ha. Seeds should be treated with Carbendazim or Thiram along with Rhizobium and phosphate-solubilizing bacteria (PSB) to enhance germination and nutrient uptake. Cotton cultivation thrives best in well-drained loamy to sandy loam soils. Prior to sowing, deep ploughing is essential along with the incorporation of 10-15 t/ha of farmyard manure (FYM) to enhance soil fertility.",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_66:003,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_66,3,"Sowing is typically carried out in June- July under rainfed conditions and during April-May for irrigated crops. Recommended spacing for normal planting is 90-120 × 30-45 cm, while under high-density planting (HDP) systems, 60 × 30 cm spacing is advised. Suitable varieties include Bt hybrids like G. Cot. Hy. 24 and 26, and desi types such as GADC-3 and GADC-4. The seed rate varies by type, with 1.5-2 kg/ha for Bt varieties and 10-12 kg/ha for desi types. Soybean Cotton (Bt & Desi) Groundnut Demo Plot Sugarcane cultivation begins with deep ploughing and the application of 10 FYM t/ha followed by ␀ield preparation by November. Planting is typically done from December to January using Eksali planting method, with recommended varieties including Co 2005, Co 5071, Co 0238, and GNS-10/11/12. The recommended spacing for planting is 75×20 cm, using top setts, preferably with a planter for ef␀icient planting. For weed control, apply Atrazine at 2 kg/ha as a pre-emergence treatment, along with two hand weeding sessions at 40 and 60 days after sowing (DAS). Interculturing should be done at 60 DAS, and earthing up should be performed at 100 DAS. Adequate irrigation is crucial throughout the",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_66:004,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_66,4,"crop's growth cycle, especially during critical stages such as germination and tillering, to ensure optimal growth and yield. Weed management includes pre-emergence application of Pendimethalin, followed by post-emergence herbicides like Quizalofop or Pyrithiobac, along with 2-3 timely hoeings. For growth regulation, Mepiquat Chloride is recommended at 45 and 75 days after sowing (DAS) to control excessive vegetative growth. Pink bollworm should be monitored from the ␀lowering stage onwards, and if the economic threshold level (ETL) is crossed, Thiodicarb or Quinalphos should be sprayed. Jassid infestations can be eﬀectively managed with sprays of Flonicamid or Dinotefuran. To manage leaf spot diseases, fungicidal sprays of Propiconazole, Pyraclostrobin, or Azoxystrobin are recommended. For soil-borne diseases like wilt and root rot, seed treatment with bene␀icial microbes such as Pseudomonas ␀luorescens or chemical treatments using Carboxin and Thiram is eﬀective. Nutrient management should include the application of zinc sulfate at 50 kg/ha and potassium at 150 kg/ha. Additionally, a 1% multi- micronutrient foliar spray should be applied at 45, 60, 75, and 90 DAS to support",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_66:005,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_66,5,"optimal crop growth and yield. Sugarcane Bajra (Pearl Millet) For successful bajra cultivation, the ␀ield should be ploughed 2-3 times to achieve a ␀ine tilth, and 5 t/ha of farmyard manure (FYM) should be applied per hectare. Sowing is recommended from late June to mid-July, using improved varieties such as GHB 905, 1129, 1225, and 1231. Maintain a spacing of 45×10 cm with a seed rate of 4 kg/ha. Seeds should be treated with Vitavax before sowing. Thinning and gap ␀illing should be done around 20 days after sowing (DAS), along with two hand weedings at 20 and 40 DAS. For weed control, Atrazine can be applied post-emergence at 1 kg/ha. Irrigation should be provided during critical growth stages like tillering, panicle initiation, and ␀lowering, especially during dry spells. Pigeon Pea Pigeon pea (tur) cultivation involves selecting appropriate varieties based on maturity duration. Early maturing varieties include GT-1, GT-100, 101, 102, 103, and Cultivation of Bajra 5150",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_67:001,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_67,1,"Gujarat ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisory 2025 Nutrient management includes application of potassium at 50 kg/ha along with recommended nitrogen and phosphorus doses, and in saline soils, gypsum should be applied at 5 t/ha. To enhance crop nutrition, foliar spray of 0.2% boric acid or nano boron should be applied at 30, 45, and 60 DAS. For plant protection, collar and stem rot can be managed with a spray of Mancozeb or neem extract around 75 DAS. White grub infestation can be controlled through seed treatment with Chlorpyriphos or Imidacloprid, and soil application of Metarhizium anisopliae or Carbofuran. Sucking pests should be managed through seed treatment with Imidacloprid or foliar spray with Thiamethoxam, while defoliators can be controlled using Quinalphos, Chlorpyriphos, or Flubendiamide. Harvesting should be done at 120-125 DAS using a blade harrow, followed by threshing to separate the pods. For weed management, Pendimethalin is applied pre-emergence and Imazethapyr post-emergence, along with hand weeding at 20 and 40 days after sowing (DAS). To manage sucking pests, seed treatment with Imidacloprid is eﬀective, while collar rot can be controlled by treating seeds with",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_67:002,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_67,2,"Fluxapyroxad or a combination of Carboxin and Thiram. In case of defoliator infestation, spraying Indoxacarb, Lambda- cyhalothrin, or a Novaluron mixture is recommended. For rust and leaf spot diseases, fungicides like Hexaconazole or Pyraclostrobin should be applied. Additionally, 75% of the recommended dose of fertilizers (RDF) should be supplemented with 25 kg/ha of Mycorrhiza and Rhizobium seed treatment to enhance soil health and crop productivity. Soybean grows best in well-drained loamy soils and should be avoided in saline or alkaline conditions. Deep ploughing and the application of farmyard manure (FYM) are essential for soil preparation. Recommended varieties include JS 335, GJS-3, and GS-4. Sowing is done in June-July with a spacing of 45×5 cm and a depth of 3-5 cm, using a seed rate of 70-80 kg/ha. Seeds should be treated with Carbendazim or Thiram along with Rhizobium and phosphate-solubilizing bacteria (PSB) to enhance germination and nutrient uptake. Cotton cultivation thrives best in well-drained loamy to sandy loam soils. Prior to sowing, deep ploughing is essential along with the incorporation of 10-15 t/ha of farmyard manure (FYM) to enhance soil fertility.",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_67:003,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_67,3,"Sowing is typically carried out in June- July under rainfed conditions and during April-May for irrigated crops. Recommended spacing for normal planting is 90-120 × 30-45 cm, while under high-density planting (HDP) systems, 60 × 30 cm spacing is advised. Suitable varieties include Bt hybrids like G. Cot. Hy. 24 and 26, and desi types such as GADC-3 and GADC-4. The seed rate varies by type, with 1.5-2 kg/ha for Bt varieties and 10-12 kg/ha for desi types. Soybean Cotton (Bt & Desi) Groundnut Demo Plot Sugarcane cultivation begins with deep ploughing and the application of 10 FYM t/ha followed by ␀ield preparation by November. Planting is typically done from December to January using Eksali planting method, with recommended varieties including Co 2005, Co 5071, Co 0238, and GNS-10/11/12. The recommended spacing for planting is 75×20 cm, using top setts, preferably with a planter for ef␀icient planting. For weed control, apply Atrazine at 2 kg/ha as a pre-emergence treatment, along with two hand weeding sessions at 40 and 60 days after sowing (DAS). Interculturing should be done at 60 DAS, and earthing up should be performed at 100 DAS. Adequate irrigation is crucial throughout the",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_67:004,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_67,4,"crop's growth cycle, especially during critical stages such as germination and tillering, to ensure optimal growth and yield. Weed management includes pre-emergence application of Pendimethalin, followed by post-emergence herbicides like Quizalofop or Pyrithiobac, along with 2-3 timely hoeings. For growth regulation, Mepiquat Chloride is recommended at 45 and 75 days after sowing (DAS) to control excessive vegetative growth. Pink bollworm should be monitored from the ␀lowering stage onwards, and if the economic threshold level (ETL) is crossed, Thiodicarb or Quinalphos should be sprayed. Jassid infestations can be eﬀectively managed with sprays of Flonicamid or Dinotefuran. To manage leaf spot diseases, fungicidal sprays of Propiconazole, Pyraclostrobin, or Azoxystrobin are recommended. For soil-borne diseases like wilt and root rot, seed treatment with bene␀icial microbes such as Pseudomonas ␀luorescens or chemical treatments using Carboxin and Thiram is eﬀective. Nutrient management should include the application of zinc sulfate at 50 kg/ha and potassium at 150 kg/ha. Additionally, a 1% multi- micronutrient foliar spray should be applied at 45, 60, 75, and 90 DAS to support",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_67:005,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_67,5,"optimal crop growth and yield. Sugarcane Bajra (Pearl Millet) For successful bajra cultivation, the ␀ield should be ploughed 2-3 times to achieve a ␀ine tilth, and 5 t/ha of farmyard manure (FYM) should be applied per hectare. Sowing is recommended from late June to mid-July, using improved varieties such as GHB 905, 1129, 1225, and 1231. Maintain a spacing of 45×10 cm with a seed rate of 4 kg/ha. Seeds should be treated with Vitavax before sowing. Thinning and gap ␀illing should be done around 20 days after sowing (DAS), along with two hand weedings at 20 and 40 DAS. For weed control, Atrazine can be applied post-emergence at 1 kg/ha. Irrigation should be provided during critical growth stages like tillering, panicle initiation, and ␀lowering, especially during dry spells. Pigeon Pea Pigeon pea (tur) cultivation involves selecting appropriate varieties based on maturity duration. Early maturing varieties include GT-1, GT-100, 101, 102, 103, and Cultivation of Bajra 5150",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_68:001,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_68,1,"ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisory 2025 This region of Gujarat includes Surat, Navsari, Valsad, Tapi, Bharuch and Narmada, SAU: Navsari Agricultural University, Navsari (Gujarat). These area experiences rainfall ranging from 400 to 900 mm annually. The soil is predominantly deep black cotton soil, which is known for its fertility and suitability for various agricultural activities. Vaishali, while late maturing ones are BDN-2, Anand Grain Tur 2, and IPCL-87119. Sowing should begin as soon as suf␀icient rainfall is received in June-July. Land preparation involves applying 8-10 t/ha manure followed by light deep ploughing and 1-2 harrowings to ensure uniform and level soil. Spacing for early maturing varieties should be 45-60 × 15-20 cm, while for late maturing types, wider spacing of 90-120 × 25-30 cm is recommended. Fertilizer application should follow a dose of 25:50:00 kg/ha of N:P:K. Gap ␀illing and thinning should be carried out 10-15 days after sowing (DAS). For managing Helicoverpa armigera, planting yellow marigold around and between rows is eﬀective. Sterility mosaic disease, which is mite-transmitted, can be controlled by spraying Propargite, Fenazaquin, or Fenpyroximate at 10 ml",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_68:002,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_68,2,"per 10 liters of water. To manage powdery mildew, spray soluble sulfur (80% WP) at 30 gm or Carbendazim, Hexaconazole, or Dinocap at 10 ml per 10 liters of water. Coconut For hybrid (D × T) palms, apply 270 g of urea/plant/month through drip irrigation. The drip schedule should be adjusted seasonally, with 1.5 hours of irrigation/day from October to February and 2.5 hours per day from March onward. Each palm should be equipped with four drippers, each delivering 8 liters/hour, positioned approximately 1 m away from the trunk to ensure even and ef␀icient water and nutrient distribution. SOUTH GUJARAT Paddy Select high-yielding or hybrid varieties suitable for South Gujarat such as GNR-3, GR- 11, GR-7, IR-28, and hybrid PRH-10. Prefer puddled lowland ␀ields with good water retention and proper bunding for transplanting. Raise healthy nursery on well- prepared land; sow 20-25 kg seed/ha for varieties and 10-12 kg/ha for hybrids. Treat seeds with Trichoderma viride or Bavistin to prevent seed-borne diseases. Apply FYM @ 5-10 t/ha and basal NPK @ 100:50:50 kg/ha depending on soil test recommendations. Transplant 21-25 days old seedlings, using 2-3 seedlings per hill at a spacing of",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_68:003,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_68,3,"20×15 cm. Maintain 2-5 cm standing water in ␀ield for 15-20 days post-transplanting, then adopt alternate wetting and drying. Apply pre-emergence herbicide Butachlor @ 1.5 L/ha 3 days after transplanting; do hand weeding at 30 and 45 DAT. Apply top dressing of nitrogen in 2-3 splits (at tillering, panicle initiation, and ␀lowering stages). Monitor for stem borer, leaf folder, and gall midge; use chlorantraniliprole or ␀ipronil as needed. For sheath blight and blast, apply tricyclazole or validamycin at recommended doses. Maintain ␀ield sanitation; drain excess water to reduce disease and pest incidence. Gujarat Stop irrigation 10-12 days before harvesting; harvest when 80-85% grains in the panicle are matured. Dry harvested grains properly to maintain quality and reduce post-harvest losses. Groundnut Use improved groundnut varieties like GJG-32 and GJG-22 with a seed rate of 100- 120 kg/ha. Treat seeds with Rhizobium culture and appropriate insecticides to prevent white grub infestation. After the ␀irst rainfall, spray surrounding trees to control grub sources and minimize ␀ield infestation. Sesame For eﬀective cultivation, land preparation should focus on creating well-drained,",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_68:004,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_68,4,"loamy soil through 1-2 ploughings followed by levelling. The ideal sowing period is from the last week of June to mid-July, immediately after the onset of the monsoon. Recommended varieties include Gujarat Til 1, Gujarat Til 2, and Gujarat Til 3. The seed rate should be 5-6 kg per hectare, with a recommended spacing of 30 cm × 10 cm (row × plant) to ensure optimal growth. Seeds should be treated with either Trichoderma viride at 4 g/kg seed or Carbendazim at 2 g/kg seed to prevent seed- borne diseases. For fertilizer application, a basal dose of 40 kg N and 20 kg P₂O₅ per hectare should be applied at the time of sowing. Weed management can be achieved through interculture or hand weeding, which should be done twice-once at 15- 20 days after sowing (DAS) and again at 30-35 DAS. Pest management for leaf roller or capsule borer involves spraying Neem oil at 2% or Spinosad 45 SC at 0.3 ml/L. To manage diseases like Alternaria leaf spot or Phyllody, infected plants should be removed and, if necessary, Mancozeb 0.2% should be applied. Water management is primarily rainfed, but light irrigation should be provided if there is a dry spell lasting more than 10 days, particularly during the",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_68:005,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_68,5,"␀lowering or pod- ␀illing stages. Cotton For Bt cotton cultivation, select recommended hybrids suited to the region, such as GCHH 245, GCHH 177, RCH 2 BG-II, and JKCH 1947. The ideal sowing period is from mid-June to early July, coinciding with the onset of the monsoon. Land preparation should involve deep ploughing followed by harrowing to achieve good tilth. Before sowing, treat the seeds with Carbendazim or Thiram at 2 g/kg to prevent seed-borne diseases. The recommended spacing for Bt cotton hybrids is 120 cm × 60 cm, ensuring a proper plant population of approximately 13,000-15,000 plants per hectare. Fertilizer application should include 240 kg of nitrogen, 60 kg of phosphorus, and 60 kg of potassium per hectare. Apply 25% of the nitrogen, along with the full dose of phosphorus and potassium, as the basal dose, and split the remaining nitrogen into 2-3 applications during the growing Pest Surveillance in Cotton 5352",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
