ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_107:004,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_107,4,"oﬀ). PLANT PROTECTION Brown spot on leaves and Brown spot on panicles Leaf blast Nodal blast Panicle blast Rice Blast: Blast is the principal disease of rice owing to its wide spread distribution and large scale destructive nature, thereby making rice cultivation impossible in many areas despite management eﬀorts. The symptoms appear on all the aerial plant of parts. Depending upon the site of symptom the rice blast is referred as 'leaf blast', 'collar blast', 'node blast' and 'neck blast. Brown Spot or Helminthosporiose Leaf Spot: The disease symptoms appear on leaves, glumes, coleoptiles, leaf sheath and panicle branches. On leaves and leaf sheaths the disease appears as typical oval or circular spots. The spot are evenly scattered all over the leaf surface. The spots when fully developed are dark brown to purplish-brown in colour with grayish white center. The disease causes black disclouration of grains so reduces seed/ grain quality as well as aﬀects the yield. False Smut or Green Smut: The disease appears only after ␀lowering and the fungus infects individual grain/␀lorets of panicle and transforms the growth into greenish spore 9190",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_108:001,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_108,1,"ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisory 2025 balls of velvety appearance. The spore balls are visible in between the glumes and gradually grow to attain a diameter of 1cm or more (double the diameter of normal grain). The spore balls enclose the ␀loral parts and are slightly ␀lattened, smooth, yellow in colour and covered by a membrane. The membrane bursts as a result of further growth and the colour of ball becomes orange and later yellowish green or greenish black. The aﬀected ␀lorets, besides having smut balls, remain sterile giving chaf␀iness to the panicle. Sheath blight Sheath blight sclerotial on stem/panicles Glume Discolouration: Grain discoloration is induced by fungal complex viz., Helmithosporium oryzae, Cercospora sp., F u s a r i u m s p . , C u r v u l a r i a l u n a t a , Alternaria sp., etc. Causing brown spots when panicles hatch out the disease leads to decrease in the number of ␀illed grains and increase in un␀illed grains. The disease appears initially as darkening of glumes and spikelets usually brown to Glume Discolouration Jammu & Kashmir Fungicidal Management: Blast, brown spot and false smut are the most prevalent diseases of rice in Kashmir. In view of the present",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_108:002,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_108,2,"cropping system, it is impossible to completely eradicate the ␀ield inoculum even after taking all the precautionary measures. As a result blast incidence is inevitable. Besides pre-sowing seed treatment and seedlings dip as discussed above, the standing crop may be sprayed with fungicides no sooner the ␀irst disease symptoms appear in the ␀ield followed by three more sprays at 15-20 days interval. However, keeping in view the devastating nature of these diseases fungicidal sprays at the following four stages of crop growth are necessary to eﬀectively control blast and other rice diseases. 1st spray at tillering stagem, 2nd spray at panicle initiation stage, 3rd spray at booting stage & 4th spray at milking or dough stage. black in colour. The glumes show rotting symptoms. The disease intensity may range from sporadic discolouration to complete discolouration of whole glumes. Such discolorations appear externally on glumes or internally on kernels, or both. On glumes, the symptoms accordingly vary depending on the type of organisms involved and the magnitude of infection. The disease has shown increasing trend in recent years in the valley. The disease also decreases the seed",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_108:003,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_108,3,"quality and aﬀects grain quality. Pre-sowing Seed Treatments: In order to minimize the chances of disease spread through seed, it is better to soak the seeds for overnight and at transplantation dip the seedlings roots in either of the following fungicides before sowing: Tricyclazole 75 WP @ 60 g/100 liter water, Ediphenphos 50 EC @ 100 ml/100 liter water,The sprouted seeds should not be treated with any fungicide as it may damage the plumule/ radicle. Field sanitation: Stubbles and leftover straw should be collected and destroyed (either through burning or deep burial). Eradicate all the weeds from paddy ␀ield as it shall help in the removal of collateral hosts of the pathogens. The best way to control blast is to integrate the use of resistant varieties with good cultural practices (proper ␀ield selection, seeding rate, fertilization, and ␀looding). MANAGEMENT OF DISEASES IN FIELD Cultural Management: Strictly adhere to the recommended package of practice regarding land leveling, soil preparation, adequate puddling, plant spacing, fertilizer and manure use, crop rotation, water management and weed- and insect-pest management, etc. as these ensure better plant growth and",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_108:004,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_108,4,"development thereby help in minimizing the disease. Integrated Disease Management: Most rice diseases can successfully be managed by use of healthy disease-free seed, proper ␀ield sanitation, application of balanced fertilizers, adherence to recommended cultural practices and, when necessary, use of biocides. Procure the seeds only from reliable registered seed agency or select only healthy and disease-free mature seeds for future use if taken from one's own crop. Before storage the seed should be cleaned, properly dried and treated with any one of the following fungicides: Tricyclazole 75 WP @ 0.6 g/ kg seed. Captan 50 WP @ 3.0 g/ kg seed. Mancozeb 75 WP @ 3.0 g/ kg seed. Mancozeb 75 WP @ 2.0 g + Carbendazim 50 WP @ 1 g/ kg seed. 9392 Sheath Blight: The disease produces spots on leaf sheath. The spots are at ␀irst ellipsoid or oval but somewhat irregular in shape and greenish grey in colour. The spots later become greenish white in centre with brown margins. Sclerotia are formed on or near these spots, which are easily detachable. In ␀ield, the spots are usually observed near the waterline. When conditions are favourable for disease development, the infection spreads up to the",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_108:005,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_108,5,culm and kills the entire leaf. Across the aﬀected parts a series of copper- coloured bands appear. The disease is also called 'banded blight'.,e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_109:001,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_109,1,"ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisory 2025 balls of velvety appearance. The spore balls are visible in between the glumes and gradually grow to attain a diameter of 1cm or more (double the diameter of normal grain). The spore balls enclose the ␀loral parts and are slightly ␀lattened, smooth, yellow in colour and covered by a membrane. The membrane bursts as a result of further growth and the colour of ball becomes orange and later yellowish green or greenish black. The aﬀected ␀lorets, besides having smut balls, remain sterile giving chaf␀iness to the panicle. Sheath blight Sheath blight sclerotial on stem/panicles Glume Discolouration: Grain discoloration is induced by fungal complex viz., Helmithosporium oryzae, Cercospora sp., F u s a r i u m s p . , C u r v u l a r i a l u n a t a , Alternaria sp., etc. Causing brown spots when panicles hatch out the disease leads to decrease in the number of ␀illed grains and increase in un␀illed grains. The disease appears initially as darkening of glumes and spikelets usually brown to Glume Discolouration Jammu & Kashmir Fungicidal Management: Blast, brown spot and false smut are the most prevalent diseases of rice in Kashmir. In view of the present",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_109:002,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_109,2,"cropping system, it is impossible to completely eradicate the ␀ield inoculum even after taking all the precautionary measures. As a result blast incidence is inevitable. Besides pre-sowing seed treatment and seedlings dip as discussed above, the standing crop may be sprayed with fungicides no sooner the ␀irst disease symptoms appear in the ␀ield followed by three more sprays at 15-20 days interval. However, keeping in view the devastating nature of these diseases fungicidal sprays at the following four stages of crop growth are necessary to eﬀectively control blast and other rice diseases. 1st spray at tillering stagem, 2nd spray at panicle initiation stage, 3rd spray at booting stage & 4th spray at milking or dough stage. black in colour. The glumes show rotting symptoms. The disease intensity may range from sporadic discolouration to complete discolouration of whole glumes. Such discolorations appear externally on glumes or internally on kernels, or both. On glumes, the symptoms accordingly vary depending on the type of organisms involved and the magnitude of infection. The disease has shown increasing trend in recent years in the valley. The disease also decreases the seed",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_109:003,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_109,3,"quality and aﬀects grain quality. Pre-sowing Seed Treatments: In order to minimize the chances of disease spread through seed, it is better to soak the seeds for overnight and at transplantation dip the seedlings roots in either of the following fungicides before sowing: Tricyclazole 75 WP @ 60 g/100 liter water, Ediphenphos 50 EC @ 100 ml/100 liter water,The sprouted seeds should not be treated with any fungicide as it may damage the plumule/ radicle. Field sanitation: Stubbles and leftover straw should be collected and destroyed (either through burning or deep burial). Eradicate all the weeds from paddy ␀ield as it shall help in the removal of collateral hosts of the pathogens. The best way to control blast is to integrate the use of resistant varieties with good cultural practices (proper ␀ield selection, seeding rate, fertilization, and ␀looding). MANAGEMENT OF DISEASES IN FIELD Cultural Management: Strictly adhere to the recommended package of practice regarding land leveling, soil preparation, adequate puddling, plant spacing, fertilizer and manure use, crop rotation, water management and weed- and insect-pest management, etc. as these ensure better plant growth and",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_109:004,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_109,4,"development thereby help in minimizing the disease. Integrated Disease Management: Most rice diseases can successfully be managed by use of healthy disease-free seed, proper ␀ield sanitation, application of balanced fertilizers, adherence to recommended cultural practices and, when necessary, use of biocides. Procure the seeds only from reliable registered seed agency or select only healthy and disease-free mature seeds for future use if taken from one's own crop. Before storage the seed should be cleaned, properly dried and treated with any one of the following fungicides: Tricyclazole 75 WP @ 0.6 g/ kg seed. Captan 50 WP @ 3.0 g/ kg seed. Mancozeb 75 WP @ 3.0 g/ kg seed. Mancozeb 75 WP @ 2.0 g + Carbendazim 50 WP @ 1 g/ kg seed. 9392 Sheath Blight: The disease produces spots on leaf sheath. The spots are at ␀irst ellipsoid or oval but somewhat irregular in shape and greenish grey in colour. The spots later become greenish white in centre with brown margins. Sclerotia are formed on or near these spots, which are easily detachable. In ␀ield, the spots are usually observed near the waterline. When conditions are favourable for disease development, the infection spreads up to the",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_109:005,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_109,5,culm and kills the entire leaf. Across the aﬀected parts a series of copper- coloured bands appear. The disease is also called 'banded blight'.,e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_110:001,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_110,1,"ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisory 2025 Sowing should be done between 15␀␀ May and 30␀␀ May. Use a seed rate of 20-25 kg/ha. Maintain a spacing of 30 × 10 cm (row-to-row × plant-to-plant). Inoculate seeds with Rhizobium and PSB culture @ 5-10 g/kg of seed before sowing. Ensure the ␀ield is well pulverized and levelled before sowing. Ensure adequate soil moisture at sowing for better germination. Apply well-decomposed compost or FYM @ 10-15 Disease Management: Maintain ␀ield sanitation. Use disease-free seeds. Treat seeds with: Mancozeb 75 WP @ 3 g/kg seed, or Metalaxyl MZ 72 WP @ 2.5 g/kg seed. Foliar Spray for Disease Management in Maize: At the appearance of disease symptoms, apply a foliar spray of either: Mancozeb 75 WP @ 3 g/litre of water, or Hexaconazole 5 EC @ 0.05% concentration Recommended fungicides sprays: Ediphenphos 50 EC @ 100 ml/ 100 liter water,Tricyclazole 75 WP @ 60 g/ 100 liter of water, Isoprothiolane 48 EC @ 100 ml/ 100 liter water, Hexaconazole 5 EC @ 30 - 50 ml/ 100 liter water, Mancozeb 75 WP @ 200 g + Carbendazim 50 WP @ 50 g/ 100 liter water, Carbendazim 50 WP @ 50 - 100 g/ 100 liter water & Propiconazole 50 WP @ 100 ml/ 100 liter water. It is worthwhile to",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_110:002,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_110,2,"mention here that the spray of same fungicides during a single cropping season should not be repeated. Earthing Up: Perform at knee-high stage (35-40 DAS) in early-sown crops. Top Dressing of Urea: Urea @ 80 kg/ ha is recommended for Irrigated Hybrid maize while the doses of urea for irrigated composite, rainfed hybrid and rainfed composite are 65, 50 and 40 kg/ ha, respectively. Apply during ␀irst weeding, hoeing, and earthing up at the knee-high stage. It is important to avoid moisture stress during the knee-high stage in early-sown crops. PLANT PROTECTION Maize Weed Control: Apply Atrazine (Atratraf 50 WP or Gesaprim 500 FW) @ 1.0-1.5 kg a.i./ha in 600 litres of water 2-3 days after sowing. Cutworm Management: Prefer early sowing (up to 3␀␀ week of April). Apply Carbofuran 3% CG @ 32.5 kg/ha before sowing. Install light traps (5/ha) and pheromone traps (20/ha) for monitoring. Use pitfall traps to collect and destroy larvae. Nutrient Management: Apply well-decomposed compost or FYM uniformly at 15- 20 t/ha during land preparation. Vermicompost @ 2.5 t/ha can substitute 5 t FYM/ha and 25% of the recommended NPK. PULSE CROPS For Irrigated Maize: Hybrid Varieties: Urea: 100 kg/ha,",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_110:003,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_110,3,"DAP: 160 kg/ha, MOP: 67 kg/ha & Zinc Sulphate: 20 kg/ha (basal dose). For Composite Varieties: Urea: 80 kg/ha, DAP: 130 kg/ha, MOP: 50 kg/ha & Zinc Sulphate: 15-20 kg/ha (basal dose). Rainfed Maize: Hybrid Varieties: Urea: 60 kg/ha, DAP: 100 kg/ha, MOP: 34 kg/ha & Zinc Sulphate: 15 kg/ha (basal dose). For Composite Varieties: Urea: 28 kg/ha, DAP: 87 kg/ha, MOP: 33 kg/ha & Zinc Sulphate: 10 kg/ha (basal dose) Armyworm Management: Uproot stubbles of the previous crop. Spray Chlorpyriphos 20 EC @ 150 ml/100 litres of water. Moong (Green Gram) Jammu & Kashmir Soybean t/ha and incorporate during land preparation. Apply the following as basal dose during the ␀inal ploughing: Urea: 15 kg/ha, DAP: 130 kg/ha, MOP: 50 kg/ha. To control early-stage weed infestation, apply pre-emergence spray of Pendimethalin @ 1 kg a.i./ ha within 2-3 days after sowing (DAS). Recommended Varieties: Shalimar Moong-1 & Shalimar Moong-2 Rajmash (Kidney Bean) Bean Common mosaic virus: Downward curling of foliage is diagnostic character in bean common mosaic virus. The leaves of infected plants become mottled, chlorotic, puckered and tend to droop, leaf size is reduced. Complete sowing in the ␀irst fortnight of",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_110:004,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_110,4,"May. Use 40-50 kg seeds/ha. Maintain a spacing of 45 × 10 cm. Recommended Varieties: Shalimar Soybean-1 & Shalimar Soybean-2. All other practices are the same as those for moong. DISEASES OF PULSES Complete sowing in the ␀irst fortnight of May. Use a seed rate of 40 kg/ha. Maintain a spacing of 30 × 10 cm. Recommended Varieties: Shalimar Cowpea-1 & Shalimar Cowpea-2. All other practices are the same as those for moong. Anthracnose: Most striking symptoms appear on immature pods although symtoms also appear on leaves, petioles and stem. Infection appears as brick red colour on the veins on lower surface of the leaves. On veins brownish black lesions appear on pods sunken cankerious spots with lighter or grey centers develop. White Mold: Appearnce of light brown water-soaked discolouration on the stem, leaves or pods and cottony growth on in collar region under humid conditions. Angular leaf spot: Initial symptoms appear on leaves as circular spots in between the veins which attains angular shape which are dark grey in colour and latter turn to reddish brown to dark brown. Cowpea Complete sowing in the ␀irst fortnight of May. Bush type: Seed rate: 60-70 kg/ha & Spacing: 30 × 10 cm.",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_110:005,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_110,5,"Pole type: Seed rate: 30-35 kg/ha & Spacing: 60 × 20 cm. Recommended Varieties: Bush Type: Shalimar Rajmash-1, Shalimar Rajmash-2 and Pole Type: Shalimar Rajmash-3, Shalimar Rajmash-4. All other practices are the same as those for moong. Rust: The rust pustules appear on all the green parts. On the leaves there are numerous small, orange brown pustules each surrounded by a light-yellow halo. Infected leaves wither and may fall. similar rust pustules as on leaves develop on stem. Diseases of Rajmash, moong, cowpea: Symptoms: The infected plants show gradual wilting and drying. Yellowing and premature drying of leaves and stems are noticed. The basal portion of stem and root region becomes black. When the bark of infected root is peeled, black streaks are seen and vascular tissues show discolouration. Leaf spot: Symptoms: Leaves show light brown small spots. Shot holes are formed in due course. Lesions develop on petioles and stem 9594",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
