ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_159:004,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_159,4,"of 45-60 cm, whereas medium and late varieties require 15-20 kg/ha with 60-75 cm spacing. For seed treatment, Thiram + Carbendazim or Trichoderma viride combined with Rhizobium and PSB culture is recommended. Alternatively, Carboxin + Thiram (3 g/kg) and Trichoderma (4 g/kg) can be used. Fertilizer application includes 25-30 kg N, 40-50 kg P₂O₅, and 30 kg K₂O per hectare as a basal dose, along with 20 kg of sulfur and 3 kg of zinc if de␀iciency is observed. Weed management involves the pre- emergence application of Pendimethalin at 0.75-1 kg a.i./ha, followed by two hand weedings at 20-25 and 45-50 days after sowing (DAS). Irrigation should be provided during critical growth stages such as branching, ␀lowering, and podding if dry spells occur. Sowing should be done from the last week of June to the ␀irst week of July, following the onset of monsoon. To manage leaf crinkle, treat seeds with Thiamethoxam 70WS (5 g/kg) and spray Thiamethoxam 25WG (4 g/10 L) at 21 and 35 days after sowing (DAS). Recommended varieties include BM-4. Apply a 40 ppm Chitosan spray at 25 and 40 DAS. For Cercospora management, spray Metiram + Pyraclostrobin (0.3%) at the ␀irst sign of infection and repeat",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_159:005,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_159,5,"after 15 days. To manage wilt, seeds are treated with Trichoderma viride at 10 g/kg, with additional soil application. For Sterility Mosaic Disease (SMD), spray Fenazaquin (1 ml/L) at 45 and 60 DAS. Pod borers are controlled using Emamectin benzoate at 220 g/ha and pheromone traps, while pod ␀ly infestations are managed with a 5% spray of NSKE at 50% ␀lowering. Proper crop management ensures healthy growth and higher yield. Imazethapyr 10% SL (1 L/ha) at 15-20 days after sowing (DAS) for post-emergence. Install sticky traps for white␀lies and monitor for hairy caterpillars, removing them manually if detected. Azadirachtin 1500 ppm (1 L/ha) is recommended as the ␀irst spray for Spodoptera, with additional insecticides as needed. For bacterial and fungal spots, apply fungicides at the ␀irst sign of symptoms. Nutrient application should follow the 25:60:40:20 NPKS kg/ha guideline, using 56 kg urea, 375 kg SSP, and 67 kg MOP. For stem ␀ly, wilt, root rot, and fungal diseases, treat seeds with Azoxystrobin 2.5%, Thiophanate 11.25%, and Thiamethoxam 25% FS (10 ml/kg of seed). Maintain weed-free ␀ields during the early growth stage, destroy host plants on bunds, and remove virus-infected",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_159:006,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_159,6,"plants promptly to prevent disease spread. Use 30-40 yellow sticky traps per acre to control white␀lies and sap-sucking pests. Apply balanced fertilizers to prevent excessive nitrogen, which attracts sap-sucking pests and promotes viral spread. For pest management, spray Imidacloprid in 10 L of water as required. To control white␀lies that transmit yellow viral disease, use a combination of Acetamiprid 25% + Bifenthrin 25% WG (5 ml/10 L water) or any recommended insecticide as per label instructions. Mung Bean Pigeon Pea 143142",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_160:001,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_160,1,"ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisory 2025 Black Gram Sowing is recommended from mid-June to early July with the onset of monsoon. Preferred varieties include TAU-1 and T9. Bio-prime the seeds with Rhizobium and PSB (25 ml/kg) for 12 hours before sowing. Apply 10:20 kg N:P₂O₅ per hectare, achieving a 50% saving in nitrogen and phosphorus. For pest management, spray Monocrotophos (12.5 ml/10 L) at the bud stage, followed by Chlorantraniliprole (2.5 ml/10 L) after 15 days. Citrus (Mandarin) SPICES Rhizome planting time: Mid-May to Mid-June (preferably with onset of monsoon or just before). Varieties: Suguna, Suvarna, Prabha. The land is prepared with the receipt of early monsoon showers. The soil is brought to a ␀ine tilth by giving about four deep ploughings. Hydrated lime @ 500 kg/ha has to be applied for laterite soils and thoroughly ploughed. Immediately with the receipt of pre-monsoon showers, beds of 1.0 m width, 15 cm height and of convenient length are prepared with spacing of 50 cm between beds. Planting is also done by forming ridges and furrows. Treat rhizomes with a fungicide solution (like Mancozeb or Carbendazim) before sowing to protect against early diseases. You can also use",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_160:002,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_160,2,"bio-fungicides like Trichoderma. FRUIT CROPS Turmeric Mandarin requires well-drained light to medium loam soil with a pH of 6.5-7.5, avoiding saline or alkaline conditions. It is planted during June-July (monsoon) or January-February (spring) with a spacing of 6 m × 6 m (277 plants/ha) or 5 m × 5 m in lighter soils. Pits (1 m³) are prepared with topsoil, 20-25 kg FYM, 1 kg SSP, and 500 g Neem cake before planting. Recommended varieties include Nagpur Mandarin, PKV Shanti, PKV Nira, and PKV Garima. Young plants need light irrigation every 7-10 days, while bearing trees require irrigation at critical stages-␀lowering, fruit set, and fruit development-avoiding over-irrigation during ␀lowering and monsoon. Fertilizer application starts with 100 g N, 50 g P, and 50 g K in the ␀irst year, gradually increasing to 500 g N, 250 g P, and 250 g K from the ␀ifth year onwards, split between June-July and September-October. Apply 10-30 kg FYM per tree annually in June. Intercropping with cowpea, green gram, black gram, or marigold is recommended during the early years. For pest control, spray Dimethoate or Imidacloprid for citrus psylla and leaf miner; Neem oil or Acetamiprid for black ␀ly and",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_160:003,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_160,3,"white␀ly; poison bait or bagging for fruit-sucking moth; and Chlorpyrifos or kerosene for trunk borer. Canker is managed with a 1% Bordeaux mixture or Streptocycline (100 ppm) + Copper oxychloride (0.2%). For gummosis, scrape bark and apply Metalaxyl paste while ensuring good drainage. Greening disease is prevented by using disease-free budwood and controlling the psylla vector. Maharashtra ENGINEERING Plant rhizomes at appropriate spacing (typically 20-30 cm between plants and 45-60 cm between rows). Ensure adequate moisture at the time of sowing. Depending on rainfall, you might need to provide initial irrigation. Pest, Nematode, soil born pathogen: Biological control - At the time of planting, apply 25 g powdered neem cake and mix well with the soil in each pit taken at a spacing of 20-25 cm within and between rows or application of neem cake @2 t/acre is also desirable. Plan for timely weed control through manual weeding or appropriate herbicides as per recommendations. LIVESTOCK MANAGEMENT In Livestock shed use of 0.01 % Deltamethrin spraying for control of ecto and endo parasites 15 days intervals. Provide ample amount of clean and cold water to livestock to avoid Colitis",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025:pdf_page_160:004,ICAR_KHARIF_STATE_ADVISORIES_2025,initial_verified_action_rag,ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisories for Farmers 2025,Indian Council of Agricultural Research,regional_policy,frozen_regional_policy_context,"State-specific chapters; this registry uses only the Haryana & Delhi, Karnataka, Maharashtra and Rajasthan chapters for compatible kharif sites",rice;maize;soybean;other kharif crops,fertilizer;crop_protection;crop_establishment,https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/Circulars/ICAR-En-Kharif-Agro-Advisories-for-Farmers-2025.pdf,2025-05-31,,May 2025,pdf_page_160,4,"disease. Use of white paint lime to drinking water tank. Vaccination of livestock should be done twice in a year against FMD. Livestock deworming should be done once in a six month. In case of buﬀalo wallowing should be done in summer season for enhancing the milk yield and to maintain the body temperature. Grooming of milch animal to avoiding the falling of loose hairs in the milk and to get the clean milk production and enhancing the blood circulation. Washing of animal specially in summer season remove the dirt and dust and maintain the body temperature and to avoid the heat strock. As per thumb rule provide animal feed should be 60 % green fodder and 40% dry fodder. In case of milch animal give 30 gm mineral mixture in case of cattle and 60 to 80 g in case of Buﬀalo should be given in the diet. MARATHWADA REGION Marathwada region of Maharashtra is comprised of Aurangabad, Jalna, Beed, Latur, Osmanabad, Hingoli, Parbhani, Nanded. The region has moderate rainfall (600-800 mm) and has medium to deep black soils. This region possesses state agricultural university viz., Dr. Vasantrao Naik Marathwada Krishi Vidyapeeth, Parbhani. Maize Maize should be sown at 75 x 20 cm or 60 x20 cm",e674cd3b6a214696d24d6876892cee6ffab3ae7e9499017f573c25690e1b901e
